spec:
  grid: 16x16, single background color (8 in eval)
  objects:
    count: exactly 4 monochromatic 8-connected foreground objects, each a distinct color
    placement: one per cardinal direction (above/below/left/right) around a shared center pixel
    center: a single bg pixel uniquely minimizing aggregate Manhattan distance to all 4 objects
    tip: each object narrows to a single pixel (the tip) closest to center, lying on the center's row or column
    tip_gap: 1-2 cells between tip and center along the cardinal axis
    shape: arbitrary 8-connected blob; not required to be monotonically tapering [observed]
    symmetry: each object symmetric about the center axis (center_c for above/below, center_r for left/right) [observed]
    area: 3-22 per object; exactly one object has strictly the largest area (the winner)
  winner: the unique largest-area object
  spacing: objects do not touch each other (min pairwise Manhattan distance >= 2)
  margin: >= 1 row/col of bg between the winner's outer edge and the grid boundary (for cap placement)
  transform:
    losers: erase each loser's tip pixel (set to bg)
    winner: add a 3-pixel cap line on the winner's side away from center, perpendicular to cardinal axis, centered on center_r or center_c; placed 1 row/col beyond the winner's bounding box

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick center pixel (r,c) with enough margin in all directions (~rows 5-10, cols 5-10).
    2. Build the winner object first in one cardinal direction, including the 3-pixel outward cap already attached; ensure >= 1 row/col margin beyond cap.
    3. Build 3 loser objects in the remaining cardinal directions; each must narrow to a 1-pixel tip on the center axis with gap 1-2 from center; give each a distinct color.
    4. Ensure winner has strictly the largest area and all objects are non-touching (min Manhattan distance >= 2).
    5. Derive input by removing the 3 cap pixels from the winner and restoring each loser's tip pixel.
